# This repository's own compose DAG. One row per `(parent, child)` pair of templates, with how
# many times the parent splices the child.
#
# Beside `rank/graph_edges.sqlc`, on purpose
#   That relation is the edge list of the graphs the model composes; this is the edge list of
#   the graph this repository composes. Same shape, and the claim is that they are the same
#   algebra: a term over named closed expressions with substitution as the only operator. Two
#   edge lists in one directory is that claim stopping being a sentence.
#
# Where the two graphs rule opposite ways
#   `splices` is where the two graphs rule opposite ways on one fact. A parent composing a child
#   twice is ordinary: the repeated relation is read once, because a query is idempotent. The
#   identical shape in `pm.part` is `checks/jagged_layer`, a violation, because supply is a
#   conserved carrier and one total closes over both occurrences. That difference is what this
#   model adds to `sql-composer` and to BPMN 2.0. `queries/observations/19-duplication.sqlc` is
#   the crossing, and it is a query because both sides are relations.
#
# Filesystem-fed and outside `pm`
#   That is a line worth knowing the position of. Everything in `pm` descends from a filed
#   document; this descends from `assets/sqlc/`. So a relation may read it to say something about
#   this repository and must never join it to a filing to say something about a subject. It is
#   the same line `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` crosses to read `pg_constraint`.
#
# The join is the point, not the scan
#   `examples/shared/tree/mod.rs` is the one scanner, shared by `compositions`, `soundness` and
#   `observations`. What a `BTreeMap` in a Rust program cannot do is meet a relation, so any law
#   that bounds one side of the tree by the other needs the DAG here rather than there.
#
# `inner_joins` is the syntax
#   Who composes whom does not say how, and the how is what decides whether a bound survives:
#   driving a query from a relation or left-joining it asks *what is missing from the whole*,
#   inner-joining it asks *does this one pair exist*, and the second hands the whole composed
#   relation to every consumer downstream. It is in the SQL text, so the program that reads the
#   text classifies it and the fact lands here rather than in a second scanner.
#
# `assets/dag/edges.sql` can go stale
#   That is the cost of the generate-then-load shape. `examples/soundness/main.rs` re-derives
#   the pairs through the same module and compares, so a `:compose` added without rerunning the
#   emitter fails the build rather than being loaded as an older tree. That is a staleness check
#   and not two independent routes: it shares the scanner, so it catches the artifact drifting
#   and could not catch the scanner being wrong.
#
# Probe
#   Run a parent that composes one child twice under `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`: the repeated
#   relation must be served from shared hits, with no reads.
-- public.compose_edge, generated by examples/compositions/main.rs from assets/sqlc/.
SELECT e.parent, e.child, e.splices, e.inner_joins
FROM public.compose_edge e
